; 23-work-with-pairs-and-tuples.kurt
;
; Work with pairs and tuples
;
; Ordered pairs and tuples (set.kurt): `(a, b) = (c, d)` exactly when `a = c` and `b = d`
; ("pair-eq"), and `fst`, `snd` give the parts back. A triple `(a, b, c)` is a tuple of its own
; ("triple-eq"), not the pair `(a, (b, c))`: round brackets around a list make a tuple. `A × B` is the set
; of the pairs with the first part in `A` and the second in `B` ("product"), and every element of
; `A × B` is such a pair ("product-pairs").
load set
const a, b, c, d, u, v, w, A, B

; pairs
use (a, b) = (c, d)
a = c ∧ b = d                    ; by pair-eq
a = c
(a, b) = (a, b)                  ; by equal-intro
fst(a, b) = a
snd(c, d) = d

; triples
use (a, b, c) = (u, v, w)
a = u ∧ b = v ∧ c = w            ; by triple-eq
c = w

; Cartesian products
use a ∈ A
use b ∈ B
(a, b) ∈ A × B                   ; by product
use d ∈ A × B
d = (fst d, snd d)               ; by product-pairs

; a function of a pair: `f(a, b)` is `f` applied to the pair `(a, b)` -- `arity f (_, _)` says
; so, and Kurt checks each use (`f a b`, with spaces, would be `f` applied to `a`, then to `b`)
arity f (_, _)
use f(a, b) = c
f(a, b) = c
